717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476828 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476828 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476828 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186476317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476828 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a1777218-b93b-5f9b-9c6b-9c80483a6a16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476859 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476859 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476859 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186476239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476859 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eed8ad9e-e0da-5240-bd75-5bd17b2051da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476879 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476879 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110989" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476879 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476879 #edb/ref 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c\\sqrt{x-y},\\, & 0\\le x-{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476879 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "18816b37-fa3d-5d53-82e0-4019189f108e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476879 #edb/ref 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x-{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/c7/c7e555ed139343a1a83e0b05bdb9a13be502271b76e9b9bbdf7ed5f3e869fcd0.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-{x}^{2}}^{x}c\\sqrt{x-y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-{x}^{2}}^{x}c\\sqrt{x-y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c[{\\int }_{x-{x}^{2}}^{x}c\\sqrt{x-y}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c{[-\\frac{2}{3}(x-y)^{3/2}]}_{y=x-{x}^{2}}^{y=x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(-0+\\frac{2}{3}(x-x+{x}^{2})^{3/2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2c{x}^{3}}{3}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2c{x}^{3}}{3}dx & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{4}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{6}=1\\,⇒\\,c=6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476905 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476905 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111166" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476905 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476905 #edb/ref 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P((X-1)^{2}+{Y}^{2}<1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476905 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6736cbc0-043f-5dbb-9f00-d4843e825805" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476905 #edb/ref 1074 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,2]\\times [0,2],$ and the event $A=\\{(X-1)^{2}+{Y}^{2}<1\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below, which is a semicircle of radius $R=1:$\n\n![](images/f7/f7f9f016d5f121b2153c1833c959be76f5a11eabb121533dd11839d6da973814.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P((X-1)^{2}+{Y}^{2}<1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{∬}_{A}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\pi {R}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476959 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476959 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110866" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476959 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476959 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $D=[1,e]\\times [1,e].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-y}{4e},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{xy},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476959 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6513aec-b650-5caf-b4d5-5b3d2bca02b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476959 #edb/ref 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$  is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the second condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{1}^{e}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}[{\\int }_{1}^{e}4xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{[2{x}^{2}y]}_{x=1}^{x=e}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}2({e}^{2}-1)y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[({e}^{2}-1){y}^{2}]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =({e}^{2}-1)^{2} \\\\  & \\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\ng(1,e)=\\frac{2-e}{4e}≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $h(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}[{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{[\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{y}]}_{x=1}^{x=e}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476977 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476977 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476977 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476977 #edb/ref 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c,\\, & 0\\le y<1-x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476977 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fbbae5a7-4143-5b60-9c21-0b9858325bf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476977 #edb/ref 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y<1-x\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/ab/ab6a4fce220150c53a70ca236843203460a7040cc1a1b5dfe664c6d45dd93ac1.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}c\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}c\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cy]}_{y=0}^{y=1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-\\frac{c(1-x)^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{2}=1\\,⇒\\,c=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
